import{_ as o,o as t,c as n,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.CS-cBh1g.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Conventions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/conventions.md","filePath":"reference/conventions.md"}'),s={name:"reference/conventions.md"};function i(a,e,l,d,c,h){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="conventions" tabindex="-1">Conventions <a class="header-anchor" href="#conventions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conventions&quot;">​</a></h1><p>These apply to the project file, the generated C++ and the app. Getting one wrong usually shows up as a robot that drives mirrored or to the wrong place.</p><ul><li><strong>Field frame.</strong> WPILib blue origin. +x points from the blue wall toward the red wall, +y points left from the blue driver station, headings are counterclockwise-positive. Robot code must report the blue-origin pose <strong>on both alliances</strong>, which is what CTRE&#39;s <code>getState().Pose</code> returns.</li><li><strong>Units.</strong> Meters, seconds, and radians in files and C++. The app shows degrees for headings and turn rates, and converts.</li><li><strong>Speeds.</strong> The runner and pose controller output <strong>field-relative</strong> m/s and rad/s. Convert if your drivetrain wants robot-relative speeds.</li><li><strong>Robot front.</strong> +x of the robot, which is the intake on most robots. <strong>Motion</strong> heading faces the direction of travel.</li><li><strong>Alliance.</strong> Routines are authored on blue and transformed at autonomous init with <code>AllianceRoutine::Apply</code>. See <a href="/Mayhem/robot-code/autos#red-alliance">Red alliance</a>. Never transform measured odometry.</li><li><strong>IDs.</strong> Action, signal and event IDs are free-form strings (<code>intake.acquire</code>). The same string must appear in the app and in a <code>Bind</code> / <code>BindSignal</code> call.</li><li><strong>Priority.</strong> Transitions are checked in the order they&#39;re written; the first true one wins.</li><li><strong>Timeouts.</strong> A watchdog timeout is a failure, never proof that something succeeded, unless the step is marked <strong>Done when time runs out</strong>.</li><li><strong><code>main</code>.</strong> The known-good version of the robot code. &quot;On the robot now&quot; compares against it, and rollback deploys it.</li></ul>',3)])])}const p=o(s,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,p as default};
